WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.250
The faculty's enrolment report

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.710
shows a hundred and twelve percent

3
00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.210
attendance in one course.

4
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:08.310
The query was correct.

5
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.160
The data was not: some

6
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.800
students were loaded twice.

7
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.800
Who should have caught it?

8
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.960
Today: how bad data breaks good

9
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.720
systems, how to test data like code,

10
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.120
and how to assess student database

11
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.780
projects built on real, messy datasets.

12
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.740
Data quality means fitness for use.

13
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.640
Common dimensions are completeness,

14
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.680
validity, uniqueness, consistency,

15
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.300
accuracy and timeliness.

16
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.710
Each can be tested automatically.

17
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.280
Write checks that run every

18
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.620
time data is loaded.

19
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.900
Keys must be unique, required fields

20
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.600
not null, and values within range.

21
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.410
A failing check stops the pipeline

22
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.030
before the report is built.

23
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.000
Better still, let the

24
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.020
database refuse bad data.

25
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.580
A primary key on student and course would

26
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.180
have rejected the duplicate load.

27
00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.090
Constraints are quality checks the

28
00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.590
database enforces for free.

29
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.320
Lineage records where each table came

30
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.240
from and what transformed it.

31
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.400
Datasheets describe how a dataset was

32
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.660
collected and its known limits.

33
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.400
Together they let someone else

34
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.840
trust, or question, the numbers.

35
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.700
Clean textbook datasets hide the

36
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.000
hardest part of the job.

37
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.090
Give students a real,

38
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.790
messy public dataset.

39
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.040
Grade the schema, the quality checks and a

40
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.860
written account of every cleaning

41
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.320
decision, not just the final queries.

42
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.130
Quick check!

43
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.720
Some students were loaded twice.

44
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.880
Which quality dimension failed?

45
00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:55.010
Uniqueness.

46
00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.610
Each student and course pair

47
00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:58.670
should appear once.

48
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.880
A uniqueness test or a primary key

49
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.460
would have caught it at load time.

50
00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.070
In the app, open the quality lab.

51
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.580
Run checks on a messy table, find the

52
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.700
duplicates, gaps and impossible values,

53
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.900
and decide how to handle each.

54
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:17.780
Your turn.

55
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.390
Two student projects return

56
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.410
identical final answers.

57
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.920
What best distinguishes the stronger one?

58
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.210
The quality checks and the written

59
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.570
justification of each cleaning decision.

60
00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.760
Remember: quality is testable,

61
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.260
constraints enforce it, and

62
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.640
documentation lets others trust the

63
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:39.980
result.

64
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.000
Try it in your next lecture: hand out a

65
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.020
public dataset with planted problems.

66
00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.470
Groups write three quality

67
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.930
checks and run them.

68
00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.370
Then each group presents one

69
00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.350
cleaning decision and defends it.
